SIIS - FEATURES AVAILABLE AFTER MOBILE APP CONVERSION
=====================================================

This document describes the user-facing mobile application after the SIIS web
application has been converted to a mobile app and the remaining mobile features
have been completed.

1. ACCOUNT REGISTRATION AND LOGIN
---------------------------------

Purpose:
Allows a person to create an account and securely access reporting features.

Features:
- Register with name, email, and password.
- Sign in and sign out.
- Maintain an authenticated session.
- Show the signed-in user's role and profile information.
- Redirect unauthenticated users away from protected screens.

2. CAMERA-BASED ISSUE REPORTING
-------------------------------

Purpose:
Allows users to report infrastructure problems using a real-time image captured
from the phone camera.

Features:
- Open the device camera from the report screen.
- Capture a road or public-area image.
- Preview the captured image before analysis.
- Retake the image when the view is unclear.
- Handle camera permission and camera errors.
- Submit the captured image with the device location and optional description.

The mobile report flow is:
1. Open Report an Issue.
2. Allow camera permission.
3. Capture an image.
4. Allow location access or use the map fallback.
5. Run AI detection.
6. Review detections and confidence values.
7. Submit the report.
8. Receive the saved-report status and report details.

3. AI ISSUE DETECTION
---------------------

Purpose:
Identifies supported infrastructure issues in the captured image.

Supported model outputs:
- pothole
- road_crack
- broken_bin
- overflowing_bin
- trash_on_road

The trash classes are displayed using the original model labels. For example,
an overflowing bin is shown as overflowing_bin and is not renamed to a generic
trash label.

Features:
- Detect one or more issues in an image.
- Display confidence for each detection.
- Display bounding boxes over the image.
- Display the native issue label beside each bounding box.
- Show a no-detection state when no supported issue is found.

4. ANNOTATED EVIDENCE
---------------------

Purpose:
Provides visual evidence of what the AI detected.

Features:
- Draw a colored bounding box around every detection.
- Add the issue label and confidence to the annotation.
- Use separate visual colors for potholes, road cracks, and trash-model
  detections.
- Store the annotated evidence with the report.
- Include annotated evidence in administrative review and notification emails.

5. LOCATION AND MAPS
--------------------

Purpose:
Shows where a report was created and helps users find nearby infrastructure
issues.

Features:
- Use the device GPS location when permission is granted.
- Show the report location on a map.
- Open the location in Google Maps.
- Use a safe default map area when GPS is unavailable.
- Search nearby issue groups by radius.
- Display issue-group markers and marker popups.
- Fit the map to the current location and nearby groups.
- Show a marker legend for pothole, road-crack, and trash-related groups.

6. REPORT RESULTS
-----------------

Purpose:
Confirms that a report was stored and explains how it was grouped.

Features:
- Show report ID.
- Show submission status.
- Show severity and priority.
- Show detection count.
- Show issue-group count.
- Indicate whether a detection matched an existing issue group.
- Show the report map and Google Maps link.

An issue group represents a distinct infrastructure problem. Multiple detections
in one submission can belong to multiple issue groups.

7. COMMUNITY REPORTS
--------------------

Purpose:
Allows users to view infrastructure issues reported by the community.

Features:
- View public reports and issue groups.
- Open report details.
- View issue types, including the three native trash classes.
- View severity, priority, status, locations, and evidence.
- Search reports.
- Filter by issue type, severity, priority, status, and date.
- Keep filters hidden until the user opens the filter control.
- Use View more and View less controls for long lists.
- Use skeleton loading while report data is being retrieved.

8. MY REPORTS
------------

Purpose:
Allows a user to track reports submitted from their account.

Features:
- View the user's submitted reports.
- Open report details and evidence.
- View current report status.
- Review severity, priority, detections, issue groups, and timestamps.
- Follow status changes from submitted through resolution or rejection.

9. REPORT DETAILS AND HISTORY
-----------------------------

Purpose:
Provides a complete record for a single report.

Features:
- Report summary and location.
- Detection list with native issue labels.
- Detection confidence values.
- Associated issue-group IDs.
- Original evidence metadata and signed image access.
- Annotated evidence access where available.
- Status history and grouping events.
- Administrator status controls for authorized users.

10. NEARBY REPORTS
------------------

Purpose:
Helps users discover nearby infrastructure problems that may need attention.

Features:
- Open Nearby Map from the mobile menu.
- Start with a default map even before GPS is available.
- Use current device location when permitted.
- Select a search radius.
- View nearby issue groups.
- Open marker popups with issue type, status, severity, priority, report count,
  and distance.
- Distinguish pothole, road-crack, mixed, and trash-model markers visually.

11. PROFILE
----------

Purpose:
Shows account information and a summary of the user's activity.

Features:
- Display name and email.
- Display account role.
- Show report totals and activity summaries.
- Provide account logout.

12. NOTIFICATION EMAILS
-----------------------

Purpose:
Notifies the configured operations recipient when a new report is submitted.

Email contents:
- Report ID.
- Submitter name.
- Submitter email.
- Detected issue types using their stored labels.
- Severity and priority.
- Detection count.
- Duplicate/issue-group information.
- Latitude and longitude.
- Google Maps link.
- Annotated evidence attachment.

13. ADMIN DASHBOARD
-------------------

Purpose:
Allows authorized municipal or operations staff to monitor and manage reports.

Features:
- View total reports.
- View total issue groups.
- View total detections.
- View duplicate-report counts.
- Review reports by status, severity, priority, and issue type.
- See native trash model class names in statistics.
- Open the administrative report list.

14. ADMIN REPORT MANAGEMENT
---------------------------

Purpose:
Allows administrators to review and update the lifecycle of reports.

Features:
- View all submitted reports.
- Search and filter reports.
- View reporter information.
- Open report details and evidence.
- Change report status to submitted, under review, acknowledged, resolved, or
  rejected.
- Review the report location and issue-group relationships.

15. RESPONSIVE MOBILE EXPERIENCE
-------------------------------

Purpose:
Ensures the app is usable on phones and tablets.

Features:
- Mobile navigation drawer.
- Nearby Map menu entry.
- No unnecessary desktop branding in the mobile drawer.
- Icon-based navigation.
- Mobile-friendly cards instead of wide tables.
- Responsive maps, legends, controls, forms, and evidence images.
- Skeleton loading for data-dependent sections.
- Prevention of horizontal page overflow.

16. SECURITY AND PRIVACY
------------------------

Purpose:
Protects user accounts, reports, and evidence.

Features:
- Authenticated report submission.
- Role-protected administrator screens and actions.
- Public community report viewing as configured by the application.
- Server-side report ownership association.
- Protected server-side Supabase credentials.
- Signed access to stored evidence images.
- Validation of image type, size, coordinates, and report data.

17. TYPICAL USER JOURNEY
------------------------

1. Install and open the SIIS mobile app.
2. Register or sign in.
3. Tap Report an Issue.
4. Capture a photo with the device camera.
5. Allow location access, if desired.
6. Review AI detections such as overflowing_bin, pothole, or road_crack.
7. Submit the report.
8. Review severity, priority, issue groups, map location, and evidence.
9. Open My Reports later to follow status changes.
10. Use Nearby Map to view other community issue groups.

The mobile app is intended to make infrastructure reporting quick for residents
while giving administrators structured evidence and operational visibility.
